      <title>CMake</title>
      <subtitle></subtitle>
      <track>Build Systems</track>
      <type></type>
      <language></language>
      <abstract>&#xA;&lt;p&gt;This talk is about the &lt;a href=&#34;http://www.cmake.org/&#34;&gt;CMake&lt;/a&gt; build system.&#xA;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA; </abstract>
      <description>&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&#34;http://www.cmake.org/&#34;&gt;CMake&lt;/a&gt; is a cross platform build system supporting Windows, Linux, UNIX, and Mac OSX platforms. &#xA;Unlike other build system, &lt;a href=&#34;http://www.cmake.org/&#34;&gt;CMake&lt;/a&gt; generates projects using the native build tools like &lt;a href=&#34;http://www.kdevelop.org/&#34;&gt;KDevelop&lt;/a&gt;, Visual Studio, makefiles, and Xcode.&#xA;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;This allows developers to use the tools they are most productive with. In addition to building software, CMake provides a &lt;a href=&#34;http://www.cmake.org/Wiki/CMake_Testing_With_CTest&#34;&gt;testing system called CTest&lt;/a&gt;, and a &lt;a href=&#34;http://www.cmake.org/Wiki/CMake:Packaging_With_CPack&#34;&gt;packaging system called CPack&lt;/a&gt;.&#xA;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;This talk will cover how to build, package and test software with the CMake family of tools.&#xA;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA; </description>
